gkey NEWS -- history of user-visible changes.

* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1.0 (unreleased)

** New features

gkey-service presents a FIDO2 (CTAP 2.0) security key to the system
through UHID.  It implements authenticatorMakeCredential,
authenticatorGetAssertion, authenticatorGetNextAssertion,
authenticatorGetInfo and authenticatorReset over CTAPHID, with
keepalive and cancellation support.

Credential private keys are ECC P-256 keys generated inside the TPM
and bound to it.  The credential list is encrypted with a key sealed
to the TPM.  TPM commands use a salted, encrypted session.

The user authenticates in the desktop's polkit dialog before every
operation, or an external command given with --verify-command decides.

gkey-service and gkey are installed set-group-ID gkey, the only group
the udev rule lets use the TPM and /dev/uhid.  They drop the group
after opening the devices, so programs running as the user cannot use
the TPM or create HID devices.  Users no longer join the tss or gkey
groups.

New program gkey lists, inspects and deletes credentials.

Both programs follow the GNU conventions for options, --help and
--version.  The build system provides the standard GNU targets and a
Texinfo manual.
